    {
      "id": "aws-secret-access-key",
      "class": "CREDENTIAL",
      "source": "(?:aws[_-]?)?secret[_-]?access[_-]?key[\"']?\\s*[:=]\\s*['\"]?([A-Za-z0-9/+]{40})['\"]?",
      "flags": "i",
      "validator": "none",
      "context": {
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      "aiisId": null,
      "techniqueId": null,
      "fpEstimate": null,
      "note": "The secret is a 40-char base64 string with no distinctive prefix, so matching it bare would flag every 40-char hash or id. It is matched only in an aws_secret_access_key assignment, which pairs with the AKIA rule so a leaked credentials block reports both."
    },
    {
      "id": "anthropic-api-key",
      "class": "CREDENTIAL",
      "source": "(sk-ant-api\\d{2}-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,})",
      "flags": "",
      "validator": "none",
      "context": {
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      "aiisId": null,
      "techniqueId": null,
      "fpEstimate": null,
      "note": "Provider keys appear bare in tool output and transcripts, not only in key= assignments. The sk-ant-api prefix is distinctive on its own, so there is no leading word boundary: a key survives an adjacent word char such as a prepended underscore."
    },
    {
